1. Install Docker and Grafana on rk010
Run these commands on rk010 (192.168.122.32). If Docker is already installed, skip the installation steps.
For Rocky Linux 9 / RHEL 9:
sudo dnf install -y dnf-plugins-core
sudo dnf config-manager --add-repo \
  https://download.docker.com/linux/centos/docker-ce.repo

sudo dnf install -y \
  docker-ce docker-ce-cli containerd.io \
  docker-buildx-plugin docker-compose-plugin

sudo systemctl enable --now docker
sudo docker version
sudo docker compose version

2. Create the project directory
mkdir -p ~/observability/prometheus
cd ~/observability

Create the Prometheus configuration:
vi prometheus/prometheus.yml

Add the following:
global:
  scrape_interval: 15s

scrape_configs:
  - job_name: prometheus
    static_configs:
      - targets: ['prometheus:9090']

  - job_name: linux-servers
    static_configs:
      - targets: ['192.168.122.190:9100']
        labels:
          instance: rk020

Create the Docker Compose file:
vi compose.yaml

Paste this configuration:
services:
  prometheus:
    image: prom/prometheus:latest
    container_name: prometheus
    restart: unless-stopped
    command:
      - --config.file=/etc/prometheus/prometheus.yml
      - --storage.tsdb.path=/prometheus
    ports:
      - "9090:9090"
    volumes:
      - ./prometheus/prometheus.yml:/etc/prometheus/prometheus.yml:ro
      - prometheus_data:/prometheus

  grafana:
    image: grafana/grafana:latest
    container_name: grafana
    restart: unless-stopped
    ports:
      - "3000:3000"
    volumes:
      - grafana_data:/var/lib/grafana
    depends_on:
      - prometheus

volumes:
  prometheus_data:
  grafana_data:

Start the services:
sudo docker compose up -d
sudo docker compose ps

Install Node Exporter on rk020
Node Exporter collects Linux host metrics and exposes them for Prometheus to scrape. 
On rk020 (192.168.122.190), install Docker if you intend to run Node Exporter in a container. Alternatively, install the native Node Exporter binary.

1. Create a dedicated service account
sudo useradd \
  --system \
  --no-create-home \
  --shell /sbin/nologin \
  node_exporter

2. Download Node Exporter
Check the latest release on the official Prometheus Node Exporter releases page. The example below uses version 1.9.1; you can replace it with a newer stable version if desired.
cd /tmp

curl -fLO https://github.com/prometheus/node_exporter/releases/download/v1.9.1/node_exporter-1.9.1.linux-amd64.tar.gz

tar -xzf node_exporter-1.9.1.linux-amd64.tar.gz

sudo install -o root -g root -m 0755 \
  node_exporter-1.9.1.linux-amd64/node_exporter \
  /usr/local/bin/node_exporter

Check the installed version:
/usr/local/bin/node_exporter --version

3. Create a systemd service
Create the service file on rk020:
sudo vi /etc/systemd/system/node_exporter.service

Add the following configuration:
[Unit]
Description=Prometheus Node Exporter
Wants=network-online.target
After=network-online.target

[Service]
User=node_exporter
Group=node_exporter
Type=simple
ExecStart=/usr/local/bin/node_exporter

Restart=on-failure
RestartSec=5s

NoNewPrivileges=true
ProtectSystem=full
ProtectHome=true
PrivateTmp=true

[Install]
WantedBy=multi-user.target

Save the file, then enable and start the service:
sudo systemctl daemon-reload
sudo systemctl enable --now node_exporter

Check the service status:
sudo systemctl status node_exporter

Expected status:
Active: active (running)

4. Verify Node Exporter metrics
On rk020, run:
curl http://localhost:9100/metrics

You should see output similar to:
# HELP node_cpu_seconds_total Seconds the CPUs spent in each mode.
node_cpu_seconds_total{cpu="0",mode="idle"} ...
# HELP node_memory_MemAvailable_bytes Memory information field MemAvailable_bytes.
node_memory_MemAvailable_bytes ...
# HELP node_filesystem_size_bytes Filesystem size in bytes.
node_filesystem_size_bytes ...

